# Docs figures = taught code (no secret plot path)

A reader who copies the Python on a Sphinx / GitHub Pages page and runs it
must get **the same figure** that page displays. If the PNG was drawn by a
different call (`direction=volume.direction`, `.data` / `label_array`, a
hidden helper, another title, or `if __name__` re-plot), the docs are wrong.
Users will assume HABIT is broken.

## Hard rules

1. **One plot call.** The `.. figure::` asset for a page must be written by
   the same `plot_*` / `savefig` the reader is told to copy — the
   `# BEGIN figures` block, or the rst `code-block` / `::` snippet on that
   page. Do not show snippet A and ship a PNG from snippet B.
2. **`if __name__` may only copy.** After the visible block writes `out/*.png`,
   `__main__` may `copy_out_figures_to_gallery` into
   `docs/source/_static/images/`. It must **not** call `plot_*` again with
   different arguments to refresh `_static`.
3. **Same objects.** Habitat overlays: `ImageVolume` + `HabitatMap`. Do not
   teach or render with `volume.data`, `label_array`, or
   `direction=volume.direction` (demo image vs ROI `+z` disagree; that flips
   coronal / sagittal).
4. **Reuse, do not restyle.** How-to / quickstart that embed a gallery PNG
   must show the **same** `plot_*` call (same `title=`, same `axis=`, same
   objects) as the gallery script that wrote that file, **and** name that
   script. Do not place a different analysis (CLI YAML, another `HabitatSpec`)
   immediately above the figure as if it produced those pixels.
5. **Regen from that script.** After changing a taught plot call or viz
   defaults, re-run the **visible** script (`HABIT_NO_VIEW=1`) and copy
   `out/` → `_static`. Do not hand-edit PNGs or plot from a private snippet.

```python
# ✅ GOOD — page snippet == gallery BEGIN figures == PNG
fig = plot_habitat_overlay(subject.image(ROI), habitat_map, title="habitats")
fig.savefig("out/two_step_overlay.png", dpi=150, bbox_inches="tight")

# ❌ BAD — page shows this, PNG was made without direction=
plot_habitat_overlay(volume.data, habitat_map.label_array, direction=volume.direction)
```

